WEBVTT

00:00:00.900 --> 00:00:02.800
[blue Merc sings an A, 220 Hz]

00:00:03.150 --> 00:00:05.050
[orange Merc sings an A, 440 Hz]

00:00:05.300 --> 00:00:06.457
ORANGE MERC: Ha! Same note.

00:00:06.757 --> 00:00:07.523
BLUE MERC: Is it?

00:00:07.723 --> 00:00:11.393
Mine shook the air 220 times a second.

00:00:11.643 --> 00:00:14.093
Yours shook it 440.

00:00:14.393 --> 00:00:17.160
ORANGE MERC: Twice as fast?
Then why do they sound the same?

00:00:17.560 --> 00:00:22.272
BLUE MERC: Good question. To answer it,
we need to know what a note really is.

00:00:22.472 --> 00:00:25.672
[chime]

00:00:26.072 --> 00:00:29.666
Every note you have ever heard
starts with something shaking.

00:00:30.016 --> 00:00:31.367
A guitar string.

00:00:31.667 --> 00:00:32.734
A speaker.

00:00:33.034 --> 00:00:35.186
The vocal folds in your throat.

00:00:36.036 --> 00:00:40.229
Pluck a string, and it swings back and forth,
pushing on the air.

00:00:40.529 --> 00:00:42.631
Here it is, slowed right down.

00:00:43.031 --> 00:00:47.534
Each push travels outward as a wave,
all the way to your ear.

00:00:47.934 --> 00:00:52.292
Count the swings in one second,
and you have the note's frequency.

00:00:52.892 --> 00:00:57.378
At real speed, this string swings 220 times.

00:00:57.678 --> 00:01:00.841
We measure it in hertz. Swings per second.

00:01:01.641 --> 00:01:03.743
Slow swings make a low note.

00:01:03.793 --> 00:01:05.941
[a tone sweeps up from 110 to 880 Hz]

00:01:06.241 --> 00:01:08.220
Fast swings make a high one.

00:01:08.820 --> 00:01:10.918
ORANGE MERC:
So a high note is just a loud note?

00:01:11.218 --> 00:01:11.914
BLUE MERC: No.

00:01:12.214 --> 00:01:14.216
Loud is how far it swings.

00:01:14.666 --> 00:01:16.102
High is how fast.

00:01:16.802 --> 00:01:20.171
MercuryPitch draws your voice the same way.

00:01:20.191 --> 00:01:23.816
As you sing,
a line climbs when you go higher.

00:01:24.716 --> 00:01:26.310
Now, back to our puzzle.

00:01:26.660 --> 00:01:28.659
Here is 220.

00:01:28.959 --> 00:01:31.232
And here is 440.

00:01:31.532 --> 00:01:33.457
Exactly twice as fast.

00:01:33.957 --> 00:01:38.396
Before you listen, predict.
Two different notes, or the same one?

00:01:38.696 --> 00:01:41.296
[pause and predict]

00:01:41.496 --> 00:01:42.896
[220 Hz]

00:01:43.196 --> 00:01:44.596
[440 Hz]

00:01:44.896 --> 00:01:46.896
[both together]

00:01:47.396 --> 00:01:51.316
Higher, yes. But together,
they melt into one note.

00:01:51.666 --> 00:01:53.238
That jump has a name.

00:01:53.588 --> 00:01:54.696
An octave.

00:01:55.496 --> 00:01:57.063
Now look at the waves.

00:01:57.083 --> 00:02:02.277
Every second peak of the fast one
lands right on a peak of the slow one.

00:02:02.577 --> 00:02:05.324
They line up again, and again, forever.

00:02:05.824 --> 00:02:09.795
ORANGE MERC: Like us walking together.
I take two steps for every one of yours.

00:02:10.095 --> 00:02:15.271
BLUE MERC: And we land together, every time.
Two for one: the simplest fit there is.

00:02:15.291 --> 00:02:16.572
That's the octave.

00:02:17.322 --> 00:02:22.323
Try 277 instead,
and the peaks drift in and out of step.

00:02:22.343 --> 00:02:24.495
That's a different note.

00:02:24.995 --> 00:02:28.136
Go down an octave, to 110.

00:02:28.386 --> 00:02:29.336
[A at 110, 220, 440 and 880 Hz]

00:02:29.386 --> 00:02:33.715
Then double, and double, and double.
The same letter, every time.

00:02:34.165 --> 00:02:39.271
The gaps in hertz keep doubling,
but every jump sounds the same size.

00:02:39.291 --> 00:02:42.189
Your ear hears multiplying, not adding.

00:02:43.189 --> 00:02:47.639
So pitch isn't really a straight line.
It's more like a spiral.

00:02:48.039 --> 00:02:52.748
Go up an octave, and you come back round,
right above where you started.

00:02:53.348 --> 00:02:57.278
Musicians name the notes on
each turn with seven letters.

00:02:57.478 --> 00:03:02.278
[eight notes: A B C D E F G A]

00:03:02.678 --> 00:03:03.954
ORANGE MERC: Only seven notes?

00:03:04.254 --> 00:03:05.404
BLUE MERC: Seven letters.

00:03:05.754 --> 00:03:06.836
Twelve notes.

00:03:07.186 --> 00:03:09.582
The other five are next time's story.

00:03:10.182 --> 00:03:14.805
A number tells you which turn you're on.
Each new turn starts at C.

00:03:15.205 --> 00:03:20.385
So the A that orchestras
tune to is A4. 440 Hz.

00:03:20.685 --> 00:03:25.634
Middle C on a piano is C4.
Same letters, different turns.

00:03:26.634 --> 00:03:29.893
Unroll the spiral, and you get the piano.

00:03:29.913 --> 00:03:33.431
The same seven letters, octave after octave.

00:03:34.431 --> 00:03:39.789
When two people sing the same tune,
they're often an octave apart. Listen.

00:03:40.089 --> 00:03:44.689
[a tune, two voices an octave apart]

00:03:45.039 --> 00:03:46.733
Two voices, one tune.

00:03:47.033 --> 00:03:51.296
So when you match a note,
an octave up or down still counts.

00:03:51.796 --> 00:03:56.767
Let's try. I'll sing an A.
Hum it back, wherever it feels easy.

00:03:57.167 --> 00:03:59.367
[blue Merc sings an A]

00:03:59.567 --> 00:04:02.967
[your turn: hum an A]

00:04:03.167 --> 00:04:05.167
[orange Merc hums an A, an octave higher]

00:04:05.417 --> 00:04:07.460
ORANGE MERC: I hummed it higher.
Does that count?

00:04:07.760 --> 00:04:12.388
BLUE MERC: Higher, but the same note.
That counts. Keep it gentle. Never push.

00:04:13.388 --> 00:04:14.334
Quick check.

00:04:14.634 --> 00:04:16.200
What makes a note higher?

00:04:16.500 --> 00:04:18.300
[think]

00:04:18.600 --> 00:04:21.379
Faster swings. A higher frequency.

00:04:21.979 --> 00:04:24.319
Double the frequency, and you get?

00:04:24.619 --> 00:04:26.419
[think]

00:04:26.719 --> 00:04:28.543
ORANGE MERC: The same note, one octave up!

00:04:29.143 --> 00:04:31.936
BLUE MERC: And why do A3
and A4 share a letter?

00:04:32.236 --> 00:04:34.036
[think]

00:04:34.336 --> 00:04:39.731
Their waves fit two to one.
Same spot on the spiral, one turn higher.

00:04:40.731 --> 00:04:41.807
Next time.

00:04:42.107 --> 00:04:46.522
Seven letters, but twelve notes.
Where do the other five come from?

00:04:46.922 --> 00:04:47.740
ORANGE MERC: See you there.

00:04:48.040 --> 00:04:53.020
[chime]

